  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1210 AM CDT THU JUL 23 2026\n\nGuidance and NHC continue to keep Tropical Storm Bertha moving\nwestward away from Alabama along the Louisiana Gulf Coast today\nand onshore again along the Southeast Texas Coast tonight. We are\nnow turning our attention to a surface front that has made its\nway into the area, currently stretching from Virginia, to Far\nNorthwest Alabama and into Northern Louisiana. Northwest upper\nflow should help to nudge this front a little further into the\nstate today before stalling tonight across Central Alabama. It is\nexpected to remain across Central Alabama through late Friday\ninto early Saturday before pushing back northward as a warm\nfront. This will keep the area in a wet pattern as it meanders\naround the area as a focus for convection into this weekend. With\nthat said, will continue the Flood Watch for the far northeast\ncounties from Thursday into early Saturday as these areas\ncontinue to indicate the best risk for heavy rain.\n\nThe heat advisory that was in effect has expired. Increased rain\nchances and a surface front moving into the area will lower\ntemperatures just enough for Thursday into this weekend to keep\nus just below heat advisory criteria. However, the reprieve will\nbe short lived. Over the weekend, the Western US upper ridge will\nexpand and nudge eastward. This will result in hotter temperatures\nreturning back to Central Alabama and only minimal if any diurnal\nconvection for the first half of next week. This also means a \nmedium to high chance that heat advisories will be needed again by\nnext Monday for portions of Central Alabama, and likely continue \ninto Tuesday and Wednesday.\n\n08",
